## Comprehensive Theory & Keypoints Guide

### I. ROUTING - THEORY & INTERVIEW GUIDE

**Theory Explanation:**
Routing is the mechanism that maps HTTP requests to specific controller actions or closures. Laravel uses a top-down matching approach where the first matching route handles the request. Routes define the public interface of your application and determine how URLs are structured.

**Key Interview Questions & Answers:**

**Q: What happens when a request comes to Laravel?**
A: Request → Route Matching (top to bottom) → Middleware Pipeline → Controller → Response → Return Middleware → Client

**Q: Why use named routes?**
A: 
- Decouple URLs from code (change URL without changing references)
- Prevent broken links if URL pattern changes
- Make blade templates cleaner
- Enable route caching in production

**Q: Explain route model binding**
A:
```php
// Implicit binding - automatically injects model
Route::get('/posts/{post}', function (Post $post) {
    return $post; // $post is automatically fetched from DB
});

// Explicit binding in RouteServiceProvider
Route::model('post', Post::class);

// Custom binding
Route::bind('post', function ($value) {
    return Post::where('slug', $value)->first() ?? abort(404);
});
```

**Q: What's difference between prefix and path?**
A:
```php
// Prefix adds before route path
Route::prefix('admin')->group(function () {
    Route::get('/dashboard', ...); // /admin/dashboard
});

// Path is the full route
Route::get('/admin/dashboard', ...); // /admin/dashboard
```

**Key Practical Applications:**

```php
// SEO-friendly slug-based routing
Route::get('/blog/{post:slug}', [PostController::class, 'show']);
// URL: /blog/my-first-post → Post model fetched by slug

// API versioning with prefixes
Route::prefix('api/v1')->group(function () {
    Route::apiResource('posts', PostController::class);
});

// Admin panel with middleware protection
Route::prefix('admin')
    ->middleware(['auth', 'admin'])
    ->group(function () {
        Route::resource('users', UserController::class);
    });

// Trailing slashes redirect
Route::get('/posts/', [PostController::class, 'index']);
Route::redirect('/posts', '/posts/', 301); // Redirect without slash to with slash
```

