### VI. VALIDATION - THEORY & INTERVIEW GUIDE

**Theory Explanation:**
Validation ensures data integrity and security before storing/processing. Client-side validation provides UX, server-side is mandatory for security. Laravel validates with readable syntax and clear error messages.

**Key Interview Questions:**

**Q: Why is server-side validation essential even with client-side?**
A: Client-side validation can be bypassed (disabled JavaScript, browser devtools). Server-side is security-critical and ensures data integrity.

**Q: Form Request vs Controller validation?**
A:
```php
// SIMPLE VALIDATION - In controller
$request->validate([
    'title' => 'required|string|max:255',
]);

// COMPLEX VALIDATION - Use Form Request
// 1. Separation of concerns
// 2. Reusable validation
// 3. Authorization checks
// 4. Custom messages

class StorePostRequest extends FormRequest {
    public function authorize(): bool {
        return true; // Can check permissions here
    }
    
    public function rules(): array {
        return [
            'title' => 'required|string|max:255|unique:posts',
            'body' => 'required|string|min:10',
        ];
    }
    
    public function messages(): array {
        return [
            'title.unique' => 'This title has already been used!',
            'body.min' => 'Body must be at least 10 characters',
        ];
    }
}
```

**Q: Custom validation rules?**
A:
```php
// 1. Inline custom rule
$request->validate([
    'password' => [
        'required',
        function ($attribute, $value, $fail) {
            if (!preg_match('/^[a-z]+\d+$/', $value)) {
                $fail('Password must contain letters and numbers');
            }
        },
    ],
]);

// 2. Custom rule class
php artisan make:rule NoSpaces

class NoSpaces implements Rule {
    public function passes($attribute, $value) {
        return !str_contains($value, ' ');
    }
    
    public function message() {
        return 'The :attribute must not contain spaces.';
    }
}

// Use: 'name' => [new NoSpaces()]
```

**Practical Validation Patterns:**

```php
// 1. Conditional validation
$rules = [
    'type' => 'required|in:personal,business',
    'business_name' => 'required_if:type,business',
    'ssn' => 'required_if:type,personal|size:11',
];

// 2. Complex field validation
$rules = [
    'password' => [
        'required',
        'min:8',
        'regex:/[a-z]/', // Lowercase
        'regex:/[A-Z]/', // Uppercase
        'regex:/[0-9]/', // Number
        'regex:/[\W]/', // Special char
    ],
];

// 3. Validate relationships
$rules = [
    'author_id' => ['required', 'exists:users,id'],
    'category_id' => ['required', Rule::exists('categories', 'id')],
];

// 4. Array validation
$rules = [
    'items' => 'required|array|min:1|max:10',
    'items.*.product_id' => 'required|exists:products,id',
    'items.*.quantity' => 'required|integer|min:1',
];

// 5. Custom messages
public function messages(): array {
    return [
        'email.unique' => 'Email :input is already registered!',
        'password.regex' => 'Password must contain uppercase, lowercase, number, and special character',
        'items.*.required' => 'Each item must have a product ID',
    ];
}
```

