## XI. QUEUES & JOBS - THEORY & INTERVIEW GUIDE

**Theory Explanation:**
Queues defer time-consuming operations to background workers. Instead of blocking the request, jobs are pushed to a queue and processed asynchronously. This improves response times and user experience.

**Key Interview Questions:**

**Q: When to use queued jobs?**
A:
- Email sending (blocks for 1+ second)
- Image processing (CPU intensive)
- API calls (network latency)
- Report generation (long-running)
- Bulk data operations (affects database)
- Any operation > 1 second

**Q: Job failure handling?**
A:
```php
class SendEmailJob implements ShouldQueue {
    public $tries = 3; // Retry 3 times
    public $backoff = [10, 30, 60]; // Backoff seconds

    public function handle() {
        try {
            Mail::send(...);
        } catch (Exception $e) {
            if ($this->attempts() >= $this->tries) {
                Log::error('Email send failed after retries', ['job' => $this]);
            }
            throw $e; // Re-throw for retry
        }
    }

    public function failed(Exception $e) {
        // Cleanup after final failure
        Log::critical('Job failed permanently', ['exception' => $e]);
    }
}
```

**Key Points:**
- Exponential backoff prevents overwhelming services
- Dead letter queue captures failed jobs
- Monitor queue depth in production
- Use Redis for high-volume queues

