## Performance Patterns

### Pattern 1: Query Optimization
```php
// ❌ BAD - N+1 queries
public function getUsersWithPosts() {
    $users = User::all();
    foreach ($users as $user) {
        echo count($user->posts); // Extra query per user
    }
}

// ✅ GOOD - Single queries with eager loading
public function getUsersWithPostCount() {
    return User::withCount('posts')->get();
}

// ✅ BETTER - Multiple relations
public function getUsersWithPostsAndComments() {
    return User::with([
        'posts' => function ($query) {
            $query->latest()->limit(5);
        },
        'posts.comments' => function ($query) {
            $query->where('approved', true);
        }
    ])->get();
}

// ✅ BEST - Select only needed columns
public function getUsersWithPostCount() {
    return User::select('id', 'name', 'email')
        ->withCount('posts')
        ->get();
}
```

### Pattern 2: Caching Strategy
```php
class PostService {
    public function getTrendingPosts() {
        // Cache for 1 hour
        return Cache::remember('trending_posts', 3600, function () {
            return Post::where('published', true)
                ->where('created_at', '>', now()->subMonth())
                ->orderByDesc('views')
                ->limit(10)
                ->get();
        });
    }

    public function invalidateTrendingCache() {
        Cache::forget('trending_posts');
    }
}

// In model
class Post extends Model {
    protected static function booted() {
        static::created(function ($post) {
            Cache::forget('trending_posts'); // Clear when new post created
        });
    }
}
```

**Key Points**:
- Cache expensive queries
- Invalidate cache when data changes
- Monitor cache hit rates
- Use Redis for production

