## Database Patterns

### Pattern 1: Safe Schema Changes
```php
// Step 1: Add nullable column
Schema::table('users', function (Blueprint $table) {
    $table->string('phone')->nullable()->after('email');
});

// Step 2: Populate data in batches
User::chunk(1000, function ($users) {
    foreach ($users as $user) {
        // Generate phone or get from external source
        $user->update(['phone' => '1234567890']);
    }
});

// Step 3: Add index
Schema::table('users', function (Blueprint $table) {
    $table->index('phone');
});

// Step 4: Make not nullable
Schema::table('users', function (Blueprint $table) {
    $table->string('phone')->nullable(false)->change();
});
```

### Pattern 2: Relationships with Constraints
```php
// One-to-Many with cascade
Schema::create('posts', function (Blueprint $table) {
    $table->id();
    $table->foreignId('user_id')
        ->constrained('users')
        ->cascadeOnDelete() // Delete posts when user deleted
        ->cascadeOnUpdate();
    $table->timestamps();
});

// Many-to-Many pivot table
Schema::create('role_user', function (Blueprint $table) {
    $table->id();
    $table->foreignId('user_id')
        ->constrained()
        ->cascadeOnDelete();
    $table->foreignId('role_id')
        ->constrained()
        ->cascadeOnDelete();
    $table->timestamps();
});
```

**Key Points**:
- Plan schema changes carefully
- Use transactions for atomicity
- Test migrations with rollback
- Document data dependencies

